    "q-163834": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163834",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14191838/assets/215b322e06280dd01e5858df9a17cd56673b5cdec69521decca205e03771ac21.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=3.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-2,1),$ so the global maximum point is $(-2,1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14191838/assets/d5f948483a80f37257b285d48cd1e6bbf1d8a6ba9b66d1fea5edd865c787711a.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
